@startuml

salt
{
  <b>MANAGE YIELD CURVE PROCESS TYPES</b> | [ Market data / Synthetic / Configuration ]
  --
  {#
    Process type catalogue | 5 types | 25 parameter definitions
  }
  {#
    Order | Code | Name | Description | Parameters
    1 | VASICEK | Vasicek | Mean-reverting Gaussian short-rate model | 4
    2 | COX_INGERSOLL_ROSS | Cox-Ingersoll-Ross | Square-root volatility, non-negative rate | 5
    3 | HULL_WHITE | Hull-White | Time-dependent mean-reversion target | 4
    4 | TWO_FACTOR_GAUSSIAN | Two-Factor Gaussian | Two correlated Gaussian factors | 7
    5 | BLACK_KARASINSKI | Black-Karasinski | Log-normal rate on a trinomial lattice | 5
  }
  [ New process type ] | [ Edit ] | [ Delete ]
  --
  <b>Parameter definitions: Vasicek (VASICEK)</b>
  {#
    Parameter | Display name | Symbol | Short label | Data type | Default | Min | Max | Order
    kappa | Mean reversion speed | κ | κ rev | double | 0.5 | 0 | | 1
    theta | Long-term mean | θ | θ mean | double | 0.03 | | | 2
    sigma | Volatility | σ | σ vol | double | 0.01 | 0 | | 3
    initial_rate | Initial short rate | | r₀ | double | 0.03 | | | 4
  }
  [ New definition ] | [ Edit ]
  --
  Shared by every type: kappa, theta, sigma, initial_rate. The two-factor engine replaces kappa and sigma with one pair per factor and adds rho; Black-Karasinski adds lattice_steps.
  --
  [ Save type ] | [ Save definitions ] | [ History ]
  --
  System-tenant catalogue. A saved row changes what every tenant's IR curve editor offers.
}

@enduml
